271
00:11:16,500 --> 00:11:17,580
Yeah, I know it's really weird.

272
00:11:17,960 --> 00:11:19,420
Most people hate them, but I love them.

273
00:11:19,740 --> 00:11:22,620
So you love being the interviewer or going to job interviews?

274
00:11:22,960 --> 00:11:23,960
Both.

275
00:11:23,960 --> 00:11:24,960
Really?

276
00:11:24,960 --> 00:11:25,960
Yeah.

277
00:11:25,960 --> 00:11:26,960
I like everything about them.

278
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:29,560
So I feel like I have a lot of passion to share with you about this.

279
00:11:29,560 --> 00:11:29,790
All right.

280
00:11:30,560 --> 00:11:34,250
So let's take a look at the first point or the first tip that you can help us out with.

281
00:11:34,620 --> 00:11:37,970
So before you go to a job interview, you should do a little bit of research, right?

282
00:11:38,170 --> 00:11:38,290
Right.

283
00:11:38,290 --> 00:11:39,250
And that's our first point.

284
00:11:40,030 --> 00:11:51,370
It's really important to know as much as you can about the company, their history, their product, their management style, just as much information as you can find out about how they work and what they do.

285
00:11:51,510 --> 00:11:52,850
And why do you think this is important?

286
00:11:53,280 --> 00:11:56,100
Well basically, you'll be asked about it in the interview.

287
00:11:56,560 --> 00:11:56,760
Right.

288
00:11:57,120 --> 00:12:01,800
And so if you show that you haven't prepared, you haven't done any research, you show that you don't care.

289
00:12:02,220 --> 00:12:11,660
One of the best ways that you can impress in a job interview is to show that you know about the company, important things like you know what kind of products they offer or what's their largest market.

290
00:12:12,600 --> 00:12:18,040
And I guess that really impresses the interviewer because it shows that you are interested in this company and they really want the job.

291
00:12:18,040 --> 00:12:21,580
And that you are willing to do the extra work it takes to prepare yourself.

292
00:12:22,260 --> 00:12:27,720
Because I think that will show the interviewer that later on when you're working for them, you'll do that extra work on the job.

293
00:12:28,200 --> 00:12:29,200
Exactly.

294
00:12:29,200 --> 00:12:29,800
That's a really good point.

295
00:12:30,400 --> 00:12:33,220
So that's our first point, our first tip, to do some research.

296
00:12:33,840 --> 00:12:34,840
Yes.

297
00:12:34,840 --> 00:12:35,840
Now you've done your research.

298
00:12:35,840 --> 00:12:36,820
You know everything about the company.

299
00:12:37,320 --> 00:12:37,840
What else should you do?

300
00:12:38,320 --> 00:12:41,600
Once you've read up on the company, you've got to spend some time thinking about yourself.

301
00:12:42,600 --> 00:12:45,180
You've got to prepare to sell yourself to the company.

302
00:12:46,080 --> 00:12:51,220
So to sell yourself, to really demonstrate them why you are the perfect person for the job.

303
00:12:52,040 --> 00:12:53,040
Right.

304
00:12:53,040 --> 00:12:56,360
Because a lot of people think about why they want to work for the company, right?

305
00:12:56,360 --> 00:13:03,920
Because they feel like they're aligned with the core values or the message of the company resonates with them.

306
00:13:03,920 --> 00:13:10,400
But what the interviewer really wants to hear is how you, the interviewee, is going to benefit the company.

307
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:11,660
What are you going to bring to the company?

308
00:13:12,080 --> 00:13:12,240
Right.

309
00:13:13,080 --> 00:13:15,580
And this is the most important part, is to know what are your skills, right?

310
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:17,000
Yes.

311
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,120
And it's not enough to just say, well, I am a great leader.

312
00:13:19,440 --> 00:13:20,440
Yeah.

313
00:13:20,440 --> 00:13:21,440
Right.

314
00:13:21,440 --> 00:13:27,000
So if you are a great leader, you should be ready to give an example of how you have demonstrated to be a great leader.

315
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:28,000
Yes.

316
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:29,000
All right.

317
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:36,920
So maybe like an example like, I'm a really strong leader because in my previous job, I had the best JD Power ratings for employee satisfaction.

318
00:13:36,920 --> 00:13:37,920
Okay.

319
00:13:38,120 --> 00:13:43,330
So you're actually showing with numbers or with facts that you are a great leader.

320
00:13:43,560 --> 00:13:44,560
Yeah.

321
00:13:44,560 --> 00:13:47,950
So the key thing here, I think, is identify what your strengths and skills are.

322
00:13:48,560 --> 00:13:49,890
Think of specific examples.

323
00:13:50,540 --> 00:13:58,370
If you can, give numbers and show how you can bring this skill and your experiences to the new company.

324
00:13:59,120 --> 00:14:01,310
And now, well, you're ready to sell yourself.

325
00:14:01,520 --> 00:14:02,270
You've done your research.

326
00:14:03,260 --> 00:14:05,450
Now you have all of these things in your mind.

327
00:14:06,160 --> 00:14:06,890
You have to practice.

328
00:14:07,160 --> 00:14:08,160
Yes.

329
00:14:08,160 --> 00:14:09,160
Right.

330
00:14:09,160 --> 00:14:10,650
A much overlooked stage in the preparation.

331
00:14:11,080 --> 00:14:12,080
Yeah.

332
00:14:12,080 --> 00:14:12,950
A lot of people think it through.

333
00:14:13,960 --> 00:14:15,830
They write things down, but don't really practice.

334
00:14:16,250 --> 00:14:16,410
Yeah.

335
00:14:16,890 --> 00:14:20,990
So I know you have a few tips about how you can practice interviewing effectively.

336
00:14:21,450 --> 00:14:21,690
Okay.

337
00:14:22,400 --> 00:14:24,390
Well, the first thing that you should do is use your friends.

338
00:14:24,800 --> 00:14:25,140
Yeah.

339
00:14:25,600 --> 00:14:26,600
Right.

340
00:14:26,600 --> 00:14:27,600
They got to be good for something.

341
00:14:27,600 --> 00:14:28,600
Exactly.

342
00:14:28,600 --> 00:14:30,160
And well, probably your friends are also working at a company.

343
00:14:30,160 --> 00:14:32,820
Maybe they're managers, or maybe they've interviewed someone before already.

344
00:14:33,360 --> 00:14:39,820
So you can set up a mini interview and have them pretend that they are the interviewer and ask you these questions.

345
00:14:40,120 --> 00:14:41,300
And you're trying to answer seriously.

346
00:14:42,040 --> 00:14:44,120
And I'm sure that your friends can give you some great feedback.

347
00:14:44,420 --> 00:14:46,360
Maybe what you did right, maybe what you did wrong.

348
00:14:47,100 --> 00:14:48,280
So that's a really good way.

349
00:14:48,580 --> 00:14:51,000
And another thing is also you can record yourself.

350
00:14:51,360 --> 00:14:51,660
Yes.

351
00:14:52,440 --> 00:14:53,440
Right.

352
00:14:53,440 --> 00:14:54,440
Yeah.

353
00:14:54,440 --> 00:14:58,960
If you have a video camera or even a web, I mean, many people have a webcam like right in your laptop.

354
00:14:58,960 --> 00:15:01,950
Record yourself actually doing the practice interview with your friends.

355
00:15:02,320 --> 00:15:09,630
And this is really useful because you can later see what you recorded and detect some of the things that maybe aren't really useful or good.

356
00:15:10,360 --> 00:15:13,030
You know, for example, posture is a really important aspect.

357
00:15:13,250 --> 00:15:13,910
So what do you mean posture?

358
00:15:14,430 --> 00:15:16,710
So the way that you are sitting down in your seat.

359
00:15:17,160 --> 00:15:18,160
Yep.

360
00:15:18,160 --> 00:15:20,320
And how you are expressing yourself with your hands.

361
00:15:20,320 --> 00:15:21,320
Yep.

362
00:15:21,320 --> 00:15:24,740
Some people, you know, they move a lot during the interview because of course you're nervous.

363
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:31,480
So maybe they're moving from side to side or playing with their feet or playing with something on the table or moving their hands in an awkward way.

364
00:15:31,800 --> 00:15:33,580
So those are the things that you have to watch out for.

365
00:15:33,960 --> 00:15:34,960
Yes.

366
00:15:34,960 --> 00:15:38,640
So I guess it's really important to sit up straight and to show that you are powerful and in control.

367
00:15:39,400 --> 00:15:41,740
And another really great thing is eye contact, right?

368
00:15:41,920 --> 00:15:42,060
Yeah.

369
00:15:42,840 --> 00:15:43,840
Yeah.

370
00:15:43,840 --> 00:15:44,840
We were talking about this earlier.

371
00:15:44,840 --> 00:15:46,400
And why is eye contact so important?

372
00:15:46,400 --> 00:15:55,040
If an interviewee maintains eye contact with me for like most of the interview, I feel like they have really good people skills.

373
00:15:55,040 --> 00:15:58,600
In my opinion, I would say they have a lot of self-confidence.

374
00:15:58,600 --> 00:15:59,260
Yeah, I'd agree.

375
00:15:59,480 --> 00:15:59,620
Right?

376
00:16:00,600 --> 00:16:01,600
Yep.

377
00:16:01,600 --> 00:16:05,480
Because if a person is looking down or not looking directly at you, it's either one of two things in my opinion.

378
00:16:05,480 --> 00:16:09,880
It's either they are not confident enough with themselves or they are lying to you.

379
00:16:10,360 --> 00:16:11,360
Yes.

380
00:16:11,360 --> 00:16:12,360
Right?

381
00:16:12,360 --> 00:16:13,360
Yeah.

382
00:16:13,360 --> 00:16:14,360
Or also they just they don't have good people skills.

383
00:16:14,360 --> 00:16:15,360
Yeah.

384
00:16:15,360 --> 00:16:16,360
Yeah.

385
00:16:16,360 --> 00:16:18,560
Which is a problem in today's sort of knowledge-based economy.

386
00:16:18,560 --> 00:16:19,560
Exactly.

387
00:16:19,560 --> 00:16:22,360
So it's really important to maintain eye contact.

388
00:16:22,360 --> 00:16:26,300
And one other really important thing related to eye contact posture is the handshake.

389
00:16:27,260 --> 00:16:31,660
When you get to the interview, it's really, really important to give a firm, good handshake.

390
00:16:32,240 --> 00:16:34,740
So what qualifies as a firm, good handshake?

391
00:16:35,180 --> 00:16:38,380
Well, you don't have to prove that you are ultra strong, right?

392
00:16:38,500 --> 00:16:41,080
So you don't have to squeeze the other person's hand really hard.

393
00:16:41,320 --> 00:16:42,560
And like break their hand.

394
00:16:42,560 --> 00:16:43,560
Exactly.

395
00:16:43,560 --> 00:16:44,500
It's not like a strength contest.

396
00:16:44,880 --> 00:16:45,880
No.

397
00:16:45,880 --> 00:16:55,840
A good firm handshake is squeezing normally, not too tight, not too loose, but your entire palm, your entire hand has to be in contact with the other person's palm or hand.

398
00:16:56,320 --> 00:16:57,320
Yes.

399
00:16:57,320 --> 00:17:01,480
Don't give them the fingers, you know, like some people give you only four fingers or half their hand.

400
00:17:01,720 --> 00:17:02,720
Yeah.

401
00:17:02,720 --> 00:17:03,720
I hate that.

402
00:17:03,720 --> 00:17:04,720
I hate it too.

403
00:17:04,720 --> 00:17:07,160
And this goes for men and for women because a lot of women do this.

404
00:17:07,480 --> 00:17:08,480
Yeah.

405
00:17:08,480 --> 00:17:09,480
I call this the dead fish handshake.

406
00:17:11,320 --> 00:17:14,000
It is kind of like a dead fish or the dead hand handshake.

407
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:15,000
Yeah.

408
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:16,200
I absolutely agree with you on this one.

409
00:17:16,720 --> 00:17:17,760
So nice firm handshake.

410
00:17:18,400 --> 00:17:19,400
Yeah.

411
00:17:19,400 --> 00:17:25,400
firm handshake and well, these are our tips for preparing for the interview, everything you have to do before it.

412
00:17:25,780 --> 00:17:27,700
I'm sure there are a lot more things out there.

413
00:17:28,240 --> 00:17:31,480
So and I'm sure our users have some insight on this as well.

414
00:17:31,480 --> 00:17:32,480
Yeah.

415
00:17:32,480 --> 00:17:34,560
I'd love to hear it because like I told you, I love job interviews.

416
00:17:35,040 --> 00:17:36,040
Yeah.

417
00:17:36,040 --> 00:17:38,640
So come to our website at EnglishPod.com.

418
00:17:38,640 --> 00:17:39,100
Leave us your comments.

419
00:17:39,640 --> 00:17:46,740
Maybe you've had a good experience with a job interview or maybe a bad one or maybe even as an interviewer, you've interviewed some really interesting people.

420
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:48,540
And you've got some great tips to share with us.

421
00:17:48,740 --> 00:17:48,900
Exactly.

422
00:17:49,840 --> 00:17:54,280
So come to our website, leave your questions, leave your comments, share your experiences with our other users.

423
00:17:54,280 --> 00:17:57,240
And Erica and I will also be there to participate in the discussion.

424
00:17:57,240 --> 00:17:58,240
All right, guys.

425
00:17:58,240 --> 00:17:58,940
Well, thanks for listening.

426
00:17:59,480 --> 00:18:00,480
And until next time, goodbye.

427
00:18:00,480 --> 00:18:09,959
Goodbye.
